::PC_U03_L04_GP01::
Question ID: PC_U03_L04_GP01
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.10
Question:
Which identity matches sin(pi - x)?
{
=A. sin(x)#Correct. pi - x is in Quadrant II, where sine is positive with reference angle x.
~B. -sin(x)#This uses the wrong sign for Quadrant II sine.
~C. cos(x)#This switches sine and cosine.
~D. -cos(x)#This switches functions and sign.
}
::PC_U03_L04_GP02::
Question ID: PC_U03_L04_GP02
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.10
Question:
Which identity matches cos(pi - x)?
{
~A. cos(x)#Cosine is negative in Quadrant II, so this sign is wrong.
=B. -cos(x)#Correct. pi - x is in Quadrant II, where cosine is negative.
~C. sin(x)#This switches cosine with sine.
~D. tan(x)#This changes the function to tangent.
}
::PC_U03_L04_GP03::
Question ID: PC_U03_L04_GP03
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.10
Question:
Which identity matches sin(pi + x)?
{
~A. sin(x)#Sine is negative in Quadrant III, so this sign is wrong.
~B. cos(x)#This switches sine and cosine.
=C. -sin(x)#Correct. pi + x is in Quadrant III, where sine is negative.
~D. -cos(x)#This switches functions.
}
::PC_U03_L04_GP04::
Question ID: PC_U03_L04_GP04
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.10
Question:
Which identity matches cos(2pi - x)?
{
~A. -cos(x)#Cosine is positive in Quadrant IV, so this sign is wrong.
~B. -sin(x)#This switches function and sign.
~C. tan(x)#This changes the function to tangent.
=D. cos(x)#Correct. 2pi - x is in Quadrant IV, where cosine is positive.
}
::PC_U03_L04_GP05::
Question ID: PC_U03_L04_GP05
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.10
Question:
Why do pi - x and pi + x share the same reference angle x?
{
=A. They are both built from a rotation of pi and the same angle x#Correct. Both angles are formed using pi and the same reference amount x.
~B. They are always in the same quadrant#They are in different quadrants.
~C. They always have the same sine sign#Their sine signs are different in Quadrants II and III.
~D. They are both equal to x#They are related to x, but not equal to x.
}